1. Introduction 1.1. Background on Head-Up Displays (HUDs) and Augmented Reality (AR)
Advanced driver assistance systems (ADAS) are increasingly integrated into modern vehicles, providing drivers with additional sources of information. As a component of ADAS, head-up display (HUD) technology projects crucial visual data onto a transparent screen within the driver’s field of view, enabling access to driving information without diverting attention from the road [1]. This innovative technology has demonstrated significant benefits across multiple aspects of driving, including enhanced performance [2], improved situational awareness [3], reduced task response times [4], and optimized driver workload management [5,6].
Recent advancements in augmented reality (AR) technology have further propelled HUD development, overcoming previous limitations in brightness, resolution, field of view, and multi-focal plane capabilities [7]. These improvements have led to the emergence of AR-HUD technology. AR-HUDs seamlessly integrates information into the driver’s field of view, merging displayed data with the real driving environment [8]. These systems show promise in enhancing drivers’ situational awareness and trust in vehicle systems [9,10].

