environment. This integration creates the impression that the icons and the road are unified, resulting in world-relative icons. These icons enhance spatial localization and environmental awareness capabilities. Compared to screen-fixed icons, world-relative icons can more rapidly draw drivers’ attention to potential hazards, offering improved safety benefits.
Recent research has challenged the presumed superiority of world-relative icons in AR-HUD systems. Merenda et al. [20] conducted a comparative study on the effects of screen-fixed and world-relative navigation icons on driver performance and visual attention. Their findings revealed that world-relative navigation icons may lead to extended task response times and potentially compete for attention resources typically allocated to realworld visual stimuli. Further investigation by Smith et al. [8] examined the impact of AR navigation icons’ spatial position, turning direction, and traffic presence on driver behavior and preferences. Contrary to expectations, their results demonstrated a driver preference for screen-fixed icons over world-relative alternatives. These findings collectively suggest that integrating head-up display (HUD) system information directly into the driving environment may not be the optimal solution, particularly in complex urban settings. The potential cognitive load and attention competition introduced by world-relative icons could outweigh their intended benefits, necessitating careful consideration in the design and implementation of AR-HUD systems for automotive applications.
Early research by Horrey and Wickens [21] demonstrated a significant increase in traffic accident risk when drivers’ gaze deviates from the road for more than two seconds. This finding underscores the critical need for drivers to swiftly detect, comprehend, and respond to vehicle crash warnings by redirecting attention to the road, thereby facilitating timely reactions to potential hazards and mitigating accident risks. Despite the importance of effective crash warning systems, current research has insufficiently addressed key parameters of Head-Up Display (HUD) crash warning systems, particularly regarding the types and display locations of such information. These factors play a crucial role in driving performance when hazards are present in the driving environment. Improperly designed warning system attributes may have detrimental effects on driver safety. Drivers may fail to detect dangerous objects in the driving environment promptly, or experience cognitive tunneling—a phenomenon where attention becomes fixated on the HUD-displayed crash warning, preventing timely reorientation to the driving environment [14,22].
1.3. Research Gap and Study Objectives
Despite the general presumption that world-relative icons offer advantages in ARHUDs, they may not be suitable in all scenarios, particularly when warning drivers about dynamically moving pedestrians. This limitation highlights a critical gap in our understanding of AR crash warning icon systems for automotive applications. Consequently, further research is necessary to compare the impact mechanisms of world-relative icons and Screen-fixed icons in AR crash warning icon systems on drivers’ visual attention during dynamic pedestrian warning processes. This investigation aims to determine the optimal application method for this specific situation.
To refine our understanding, we have further categorized AR-HUD crash warning icons into three distinct types (Figure 1): Based on Driver’s view (BD): Presents an icon directly in the driver’s line of sight when a hazardous pedestrian is detected, alerting them to the danger on the road. Based on Road information (BR): Projects an icon onto the road surface ahead of the vehicle upon detecting a hazard. Based on Warning information (BW): Displays the icon at the location of the hazardous pedestrian and moves along with the pedestrian to continuously indicate the danger.
