To test this hypothesis, we conducted an experiment to acquire drivers’ eye movement data during simulated driving based on 360-degree video demonstrations. This approach allows us to understand the dynamic visual attention mechanisms of drivers. The utilization of 360-degree video-based simulated driving environments provides a comprehensive and immersive platform for examining drivers’ visual behaviors. By capturing eye movement data within this controlled yet realistic setting, we can gain valuable insights into the dynamic nature of drivers’ visual attention allocation.
Eye-tracking technology is highly valuable in studying drivers’ visual attention patterns. Previous research has utilized this technology to analyze the impact of Advanced Driver Assistance Systems (ADAS) on driving behavior. For example, Falkmer and Gregersen [23] used eye-tracking data to compare visual search strategies between experienced and inexperienced drivers, revealing that the latter focused more on in-car objects and traffic cues. Li et al. [24] assessed the usability and safety effects of different in-vehicle display systems across various speeds, measuring average gaze duration, number of gazes, and blink rate. Hajiseyedjavadi et al. [25] investigated how warning icons influence young drivers’ hazard perception and visual attention. Similarly, Yang et al. [26] studied the impact of warning icon placements on driving performance and visual patterns in different traffic accident scenarios. By analyzing eye-tracking data during alert notifications, researchers can gain insights into the visual attention mechanisms underlying driving behavior, thus improving the design and safety of warning systems.
2. Methods 2.1. Warning Icon Types
This study explores the influence of three types of warning icons on drivers’ visual behavior in various road-turning scenarios during vehicular accidents. These icons are depicted in red (RGB 255, 0, 0) due to their universally recognized association with caution and danger, as evidenced in the existing literature. The design of the pedestrian warning icons aligns with the style used in the research conducted by Winkler et al. [16].
