to the warning functions of the HUD system and provide detailed explanations of the driving simulation experiment procedures and tasks. The research team assists participants in properly wearing and calibrating the HTC VIVE virtual reality eye-tracking device, adjusting the equipment to ensure the collection of valid eye movement data. Participants then undergo a 2-km driving simulation training session to familiarize themselves with the virtual urban road environment and human-vehicle interaction event processes. This training phase contributes to enhancing the quality of experimental data.
Experimental Phase. During the formal experimental phase, participants experience three driving video segments containing potential hazardous events. Brief rest periods are provided between each video segment, with the order of presentation randomized. Subjects are required to maintain a vehicle speed of 60 km/h before the occurrence of hazardous events in the videos. Upon detecting a potential danger ahead, the vehicle automatically activates its collision warning system, initiates automatic braking, and displays a warning icon on the windshield. Drivers must immediately locate the position of the hazardous object in the scene after the icon appears.

