dents. Multiple metrics, including total gaze duration, number of fixation points, and time to first fixation, indicate that the hazard-based warning icon display type (BW) significantly enhances drivers’ attention to dangerous pedestrians in the driving environment compared to the other two warning icon display types (BD, BR). This optimization of drivers’ visual attention allocation generally supports the H1 hypothesis, which posited significant differences in the effectiveness of various AR warning icon types for drivers’ perception of potential hazards. As hypothesized, the BW-type warning icons most effectively facilitated hazard recognition. This finding aligns with the results of Calvi et al. [30], demonstrating that dynamic, location-based warnings (like our BW condition) lead to faster hazard detection times and more accurate responses compared to static or dashboard-based warnings. Similarly, the study by Lindemann et al. (2021) corroborates the conclusion that dynamic AR warnings are more effective than fixed-position warnings [31]. The results underscore the superiority of hazard-based AR warning icons in enhancing drivers’ situational awareness and response to potential pedestrian-related dangers. This research contributes to the growing body of evidence supporting the implementation of dynamic, context-aware warning systems in automotive safety technology.
As hypothesized, the BW-type warning icons most effectively facilitated hazard recognition. Multiple metrics, including total gaze duration, number of fixation points, and time to first fixation, indicate that BW significantly enhances drivers’ attention to dangerous pedestrians in the driving environment compared to the other two warning icon display types (BD, BR). This optimization of drivers’ visual attention allocation fully aligns with hypothesis H1c, which stated that “AR warning icons based on warnings (BW) displayed at the location of dangerous pedestrians and moving with them will most effectively promote hazard recognition, being more effective than BD and BR displays”. Previous research findings suggest that since the display positions of BD- and BR-type warning information do not change with the movement of dangerous pedestrians, these warning icons compete with potential hazards in the driving scene for drivers’ attentional resources [20,22]. Consequently, during pedestrian-vehicle interactions, drivers’ gaze duration on dangerous pedestrians is shorter, as they allocate a portion of their attention to the warning icons, resulting in decreased focus on potential risks in the scene. The superior performance of BW-type icons can be attributed to their dynamic nature, which aligns the warning with the actual location of the hazard. This alignment reduces the cognitive load on drivers, allowing them to more efficiently process and respond to potential dangers. The findings underscore the importance of context-aware, spatially-congruent warning systems in enhancing driver safety, particularly in complex urban environments with high pedestrian activity.
The research findings partially support hypothesis H2, which posited significant differences in the impact of various turning lane types on drivers’ visual behavior and hazard perception. During turning maneuvers, the BW group demonstrated significantly shorter time to first fixation compared to the BD and BR groups. This indicates that the BWtype warning icon display method more effectively captures drivers’ attention, enabling them to swiftly detect potential hazards and make decisions, thereby ensuring driving safety. In straight lane scenarios, no significant differences were observed in the time to first fixation across the three icon types. This suggests that in this context, drivers exhibit strong perception of dangerous pedestrians, unaffected by the warning icon type. The longer time to first fixation in the BD and BR groups implies that after the warning icons appear, drivers focus on the icons themselves, unable to promptly return their attention to the driving environment. This phenomenon, known as cognitive tunneling, aligns with previous research findings [14]. Moreover, due to the relatively fixed positions of BD and BR icons, drivers need to search for hazardous objects in the forward road environment after the icon appears. This process may lead to an extended time to first fixation on the hazardous objects for drivers in these groups.
These results underscore the context-dependent effectiveness of different AR warning icon types. The BW-type icons demonstrate superior performance in more complex driving scenarios, such as turning maneuvers, where rapid hazard detection is crucial. However, in

