simpler straight-lane driving, the impact of icon type appears to be less pronounced. This nuanced understanding of icon effectiveness across various driving contexts can inform the design of more adaptive and efficient AR-HUD systems, potentially enhancing overall driving safety in diverse urban environments.
Consistent with the initial hypotheses, significant differences were observed in the eye movement data of the BD and BR groups across various driving lane conditions. This indicates that under BD and BR conditions, the visual field differences caused by turning lanes affect drivers’ perception of dangerous pedestrians. In other words, under these two types of warning icon conditions, drivers are influenced by the vehicle’s driving lane. Moreover, executing turning maneuvers increases the complexity of the driving task. Drivers must respond to the current traffic situation of the turning road, rather than simply maintaining longitudinal and lateral vehicle control [32]. Our research findings partially support Wang et al.’s observations, particularly regarding the potential for BD- and BR-type warnings to cause distraction [33]. However, our study further demonstrates that BW-type warnings maintain consistent effectiveness across different driving scenarios, forming an interesting contrast with Wang et al.’s research. This discrepancy may stem from our implementation of dynamic, context-aware AR warning designs, which appear to better balance information presentation and attention management. The BW-type icons, by adapting to the driving environment and potential hazards, seem to mitigate the lane-dependent effects observed with the BD and BR types. This adaptive approach potentially reduces cognitive load on drivers, allowing for more efficient processing of critical safety information regardless of the specific driving lane or maneuver being executed. These findings underscore the importance of considering the interaction between warning icon design and driving context in the development of AR-HUD systems. The superior performance of the BW-type icons across various driving scenarios suggests that future AR warning systems should prioritize dynamic, hazard-based designs to enhance driver safety and situational awareness in complex urban environments.
In this study, drivers demonstrated a higher ability to detect hazardous objects and maintain increased attention on straight roads without turning tasks compared to leftturn and right-turn lanes. For the based on warning (BW) warning icon, no significant differences were observed in drivers’ eye movement data across various driving lanes. This suggests that under BW conditions, driver performance remains consistent regardless of the lane, with relatively stable behavior across different driving lanes and minimal variations due to differences in visual fields during turning maneuvers. Although no significant differences were found in subjective satisfaction ratings among the three icon types, the BW icon received higher satisfaction scores compared to the other two icon types.
While the results have addressed the research objectives, this research still has limitations. The participant pool in this study was relatively homogeneous, primarily consisting of young adults with limited driving experience. Consequently, the findings may only represent characteristics specific to this demographic group. Research indicates that as drivers age, their abilities in attention allocation, environmental awareness, information processing, and decision-making may gradually decline [27,34,35]. Conversely, extensive driving experience can enhance drivers’ ability to perceive hazardous objects [28,36–38], potentially reducing collision rates. Future research should explore the applicability of augmented reality (AR) icons across drivers of varying ages, driving experiences, and cognitive levels. This expanded scope would provide a more comprehensive understanding of the effectiveness of AR icons in diverse driver populations.
This study focused solely on emergency situations involving vehicle-pedestrian accidents. However, real-world driving environments are considerably more complex, with various hazardous objects exhibiting different velocities, motion states, spatial positions, and other characteristics. To optimize the application of driver assistance systems in promoting driving safety more effectively, future research should consider a more nuanced categorization of accident types. Subsequent studies could explore the applicability of augmented reality (AR) warning information displays across diverse accident scenarios.

