crash warning icon systems, with the goal of minimizing driver distraction and enhancing the detection of potential hazards in pedestrian-vehicle scenarios.
The findings reveal that AR crash warning icon systems based on hazardous objects enable drivers to identify dangerous pedestrians in the environment more rapidly. Moreover, this type of AR icon demonstrates consistent visual behavior across various turning lanes, unaffected by differences in field of view, indicating strong applicability. In contrast, screen-fixed AR crash warning icon systems may divert drivers’ attention, competing with potential risks in the environment for limited visual resources.
These findings specifically indicate that when designing AR crash warning icon systems, priority should be given to the dynamic tracking capability of the icons. This ensures that icons can move and update with hazardous objects, helping drivers identify and respond to potential threats in the driving environment accurately and promptly. Furthermore, the study emphasizes avoiding the use of screen-fixed icon types to reduce competition for visual resources and minimize distraction, thereby enhancing overall safety during driving.
Future research should explore the impact of multiple concurrent warning signals on drivers, including the following: (a) Investigating responses of diverse driver populations (age, experience, cognitive levels) to AR icons, enhancing generalizability. (b) Examining AR warning displays across various accident scenarios. (c) Designing experiments that closely replicate real-world driving for improved ecological validity. (d) Studying the effects of multiple warning signals on driver performance and cognitive load. These directions would address limitations and provide a comprehensive understanding of AR warning systems in diverse driving scenarios, contributing to the development of effective, universally applicable driver assistance technologies.
